('f_shear_029','m_shear','His most recent X post, 2026-08-31T16:30:53Z — three days before this run: "The difference between things created via symbiogenesis and synbiogenesis is the difference between natural and artificial things."','self_published','subject_authored','https://x.com/eshear','x.com','2026-08-31','2026-09-04T01:45:00Z',NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'run_ingest_shear_20260903'),

('f_shear_030','m_shear','On the models he uses, 2026-07-27: "Talking to Opus makes me angry and depressed in a way that''s hard to articulate. It''s actually somehow worse than Sol. Fable is still a breath of fresh air in comparison, even if it does have the worst llm-babble tendencies." His most-read post of the period, 69,000 views.','self_published','subject_authored','https://x.com/eshear','x.com','2026-07-27','2026-09-04T01:45:00Z',NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'run_ingest_shear_20260903'),

('f_shear_031','m_shear','2026-06-16: "Being precise in your use of language is quite different from being accurate. And both are different from being efficient." He follows it immediately: "I value precise use of language a lot. I will admit, perhaps even too much."','self_published','subject_authored','https://x.com/eshear','x.com','2026-06-16','2026-09-04T01:45:00Z',NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'run_ingest_shear_20260903'),

('f_shear_032','m_shear','2026-05-30, captioned "Want to upgrade your ontology of mind?", he photographed a stack of books on his desk. Titles legible on the spines, top to bottom: What Is Intelligence?; Sex, Ecology, Spirituality (Wilber); After Virtue (MacIntyre); Statistical Physics 3rd Edition Part 1; Zen And The Art Of Motorcycle Maintenance; Life Itself; The Origins Of Order (Kauffman); Decoherence and Quantum Darwinism (Zurek); The Classical Theory of Fields. Four of the nine also appear by name on his own edbs.media book pages — an image corroborating a text, not standing on its own.','self_published','subject_authored','https://x.com/eshear','x.com','2026-05-30','2026-09-04T01:45:00Z',NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'run_ingest_shear_20260903'),

('f_shear_033','m_shear','Cadence, measured on the logged-in timeline: 23 posts between 2026-05-15 and 2026-08-31, roughly two a week, unbroken. Five of those are also readable LOGGED OUT — the profile shell renders the newest handful to an anonymous browser even though /following does not. He is active, not quiet and not unknown.','self_published','subject_authored','https://x.com/eshear','x.com','2026-08-31','2026-09-04T01:45:00Z',NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'run_ingest_shear_20260903'),

('f_shear_034','m_shear','Follow graph, PARTIAL: 70 of the 1,193 accounts he follows (5.9%) were reached in two full passes through x.com/eshear/following on the operator''s logged-in Chrome, with real wheel events and every selector scoped to [data-testid="primaryColumn"]. The second pass, after a reload, returned a strict subset of the first — the same silent ceiling measured on Wilson (69 of 1,345): no spinner, no error, no 429, the list simply ends at @NoCommas. What is in the reachable 70: @mwseibel (Michael Seibel), @justinkan, @kvogt (Kyle Vogt) — three of his four Justin.tv co-founders; @AskYatharth (Yatharth Agarwal, Softmax); @tlbtlbtlb (Trevor Blackwell, YC); and exactly one of the other nine members, @nabeelqu. The rest is overwhelmingly AI research and physics: @drfeifei, @carlorovelli, @naval, @danielgross, @SakanaAILabs, @thinkymachines, @cerebras, @froystig, @yaroslavvb, @SchreiberUrs, @AlexKontorovich.','self_published','subject_authored','https://x.com/eshear/following','x.com','2026-09-04','2026-09-04T01:45:00Z',NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'run_ingest_shear_20260903'),

-- ── ThreadReader: the workaround that makes 18 long-form threads readable ─────
('f_shear_035','m_shear','The amateur physics is real and he labels it himself. Thread of 2025-08-11: "Since the cool kids are doing it, my quantum gravity prediction below! Epistemic warning: crackpot physics from someone who isn''t a physicist. Epistemic upside: I think I have one maybe actually correct idea buried in it." Then straight in: "Ok, so there''s just one quantum field. Likely in C^4 interacting via CP^3 ala Twistors or teleparallel gravity, so we''ll go with that. A ''particle'' excitation in this field is a probability density, basically a (mixed-state) spinor." 21 tweets.','self_published','subject_authored','https://threadreaderapp.com/user/eshear','threadreaderapp.com','2025-08-11','2026-09-04T01:45:00Z',NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'run_ingest_shear_20260903'),

('f_shear_036','m_shear','2025-06-20, 25 tweets: "I found an old list of blog post ideas that I will probably never write, but I thought it would be fun to turn them into a thread. I wrote these years ago, fun to see the trajectory of my journey. I find them all delightful, even if some are wrong in retrospect." The first one out of the drawer: "Power is like radioactive ore…drives the engine of an organization but dangerous to everyone who touches it. Needs to be contained and channeled."','self_published','subject_authored','https://threadreaderapp.com/user/eshear','threadreaderapp.com','2025-06-20','2026-09-04T01:45:00Z',NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'run_ingest_shear_20260903'),

('f_shear_037','m_shear','On the NPC theory, 2025-09-28: "''most people'' don''t really have deep inner lives where they yearn for meaning and purpose, they''re just incentive-following automatons... It''s paired with a second, usually unstated claim. Most people are NPCs, but not us…not the ones having this discussion. We are the special ones, the real people who actually care about the world." And on dunking, 2025-08-22: "Every time you dunk on someone''s stupid, evil, unacceptable behavior and message, imagine yourself serving them... Attention is care, energy, fuel: starve them out."','self_published','subject_authored','https://threadreaderapp.com/user/eshear','threadreaderapp.com','2025-09-28','2026-09-04T01:45:00Z',NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'run_ingest_shear_20260903'),

('f_shear_038','m_shear','The sharpest thing he has written about a company that is not his, 2025-07-24: "Google exists bc of a grand bargain: scrape the open web, and profit from directing traffic to the best sites. Around 2010, the betrayal began. YouTube artificially ranked above other video, then over time maps results injected, shopping, flights, events. Now AI answers." He finishes: "while Google makes billions in the short run, they''re systematically destroying the very foundations of their own business and have been for a decade. Google is cancer."','self_published','subject_authored','https://threadreaderapp.com/user/eshear','threadreaderapp.com','2025-07-24','2026-09-04T01:45:00Z',NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'run_ingest_shear_20260903'),

('f_shear_039','m_shear','He takes apart a widely-shared study, 2025-07-14, 12 tweets: "METR''s analysis of this experiment is wildly misleading. The results indicate that people who have ~never used AI tools before are less productive while learning to use the tools, and say ~nothing about experienced AI tool users... I immediately found the claim suspect because it didn''t jibe with my own experience working w people using coding assistants, but sometimes there are surprising results so I dug in."','self_published','subject_authored','https://threadreaderapp.com/user/eshear','threadreaderapp.com','2025-07-14','2026-09-04T01:45:00Z',NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'run_ingest_shear_20260903'),

('f_shear_040','m_shear','ThreadReader holds 18 of his threads, newest 2025-11-24, which is a workaround for the timeline rather than a limit on him. The 2025-11-24 one: "As someone w a computer science background, I was very confused the first time I encountered math ppl insisting a tensor wasn''t a matrix. Bc they are both clearly multidimensional arrays. But then I realized…mathematicians always use strongly typed languages in their heads!" The two-part "greater theory of system design" (2025-06-24 and 2025-07-06, 19 and 23 tweets) is the longest thing he wrote in 2025.','self_published','subject_authored','https://threadreaderapp.com/user/eshear','threadreaderapp.com','2025-11-24','2026-09-04T01:45:00Z',NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'run_ingest_shear_20260903'),

-- ── blog.emmettshear.com, "Optimize Prime" — the SUBDOMAIN, Wayback only ──────
('f_shear_041','m_shear','His first blog was called "Optimize Prime" and lived at blog.emmettshear.com — the subdomain; the apex is not his and the eshear.com domain never was. Wayback''s index carries posts from 2006-08-21 to 2010-02-12, signed "By Emmett", tagged code / business / ebay / education / book review / facts. The titles are a 23-year-old founder in public: "The Kiko Asset Sale", "The Kiko Asset Sale: Finished", "Lessons from eBaying Kiko", "The 100k Plan", "Blacklist Script for Reddit", "Arrival in San Francisco", "Yogurt: The Unknown Danger", "Things I Didn''t Know About The History Of Schools", "What''s Wealth", "Splay Tree", "Don''t use Pound for load balancing", "Counting Uniques With MongoDB".','self_published','subject_authored','http://web.archive.org/web/20061206190139/http://blog.emmettshear.com:80/','blog.emmettshear.com','2006-12-06','2026-09-04T01:45:00Z',NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'run_ingest_shear_20260903'),

('f_shear_042','m_shear','From that blog, 2006-11-30, the whole post titled "This Blog Is Boring": "I think that''s objectively true. What I''m really worried about is that this reflects my current personality, working for a startup: boring. More generally, I''ve been concerned that everyone in startups winds up, to an outsider, fundamentally boring. The startups in Crystal Towers (we need a collective name - suggestions) were discussing this a few days ago. Someone noted that when they went home they had no idea what to talk about with people, if they couldn''t discuss work... My only hope is to retire to a monastery in the mountains."','self_published','subject_authored','http://web.archive.org/web/20061206190139/http://blog.emmettshear.com:80/','blog.emmettshear.com','2006-11-30','2026-09-04T01:45:00Z',NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'run_ingest_shear_20260903'),

('f_shear_043','m_shear','2006-10-26, "That''s very liberal of you" — twenty years before the NPC thread, the same move: "I recently overheard something here in San Francisco that disturbed me. Someone said ''He helped me set up the event; he''s a real liberal.'' Liberal here is being used in the same sense as Christian; a place holder for ''good''. This is disturbing because it implies that the members of this culture actually believe that conservatives lack personal, positive traits. That''s simply not true, and it''s foolish to think otherwise... Neither Christians nor liberals have any monopoly on kindness."','self_published','subject_authored','http://web.archive.org/web/20071224103940/http://blog.emmettshear.com:80/post/2006/10/26/Thats-very-liberal-of-you','blog.emmettshear.com','2006-10-26','2026-09-04T01:45:00Z',NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'run_ingest_shear_20260903'),

('f_shear_044','m_shear','That 2006 post drew a two-comment argument, and the first comment is signed "Aaron Swartz": "I think that''s an act of radical misinterpretation followed by some unjustified conclusions. When I''ve heard people speak this way, they mean ''liberal'' in a self-deprecating, I''m-ashamed-of-my-own-liberal-guilt kind of way... If I say, ''He gave that rich ugly woman a hug; that was really mercenary of him.'' does that imply there are non-mercenaries can''t hug? Come on." Shear answers in the thread. IDENTITY NOT ESTABLISHED: a blog comment name field is a display name and nothing else, so this is one WEAK signal on a forgeable surface and does not meet R-012. Traversal hint only; no person row and no edge was written for it.','third_party','third_party_open','http://web.archive.org/web/20071224103940/http://blog.emmettshear.com:80/post/2006/10/26/Thats-very-liberal-of-you','blog.emmettshear.com','2006-10-26','2026-09-04T01:45:00Z',NULL,0,NULL,NULL,'run_ingest_shear_20260903'),

